over the past few decades bonds have  fallen out of favor the average person  isn't even aware what exactly bonds are  while investors avoid them like the  plague  this is quite ironic given that the  biggest companies in the world often do  the exact opposite holding tens of  billions if not hundreds of billions in  bonds for example we often hear that  apple has nearly 200 billion dollars in  cash but this is rather misleading  because they don't actually hold  anywhere near this amount in cash  apple actually only holds 28 billion  dollars in cash  the rest of apple's money is held in  what are called marketable securities  which are basically just bonds and other  fixed income assets  apple currently holds 23 billion  short-term marketable securities and 141  billion dollars in long-term marketable  securities in fact a few years ago apple  was the 23rd largest us treasury  investor in the world beating out most  countries and if you take a look at the  other leading companies in the world  you'll find a very similar story google  for instance is currently holding 113  billion dollars in marketable securities  so here's why these companies hold the  vast majority of their cash in  marketable securities and why all  americans should be investing in bonds  as well  before we jump into why bonds could be a  favorable investment let's first recap  what exactly bonds even are in the first  place bonds are basically iou statements  handed out by governments and  corporations around the world whenever  these entities need money to build a  road or a new factory they'll issue  these iou statements and they'll promise  to pay a certain amount of interest  until they pay back the full amount so  you as a bondholder are basically a  loaning agency to these massive entities  usually the vast majority of bonds are  sold to institutions like pension funds  and other countries like china but this  isn't on purpose  in fact governments around the world  would prefer to issue their bonds to  their citizens this way they're paying  interest to their citizens instead of  china or russia most governments  actually go out of their way to ensure  that their bonds are easily accessible  to the average person the us government  for example guarantees that they'll  offer you at least 5 million worth of  each bond every single time they issue  bonds  in other words you don't have to compete  with the big institutions and countries  unless you're buying more than 5 million  worth of treasury bonds on a regular  basis it's not just the federal  governments that are issuing bonds  either  usually most areas will issue their own  bonds to fund a local dam or highway  these are called municipal bonds and  they're a great way to earn money while  supporting your local communities also  interest payments earned from municipal  bonds are exempt from federal income  taxes and sometimes also state and local  taxes as well and if you live in a high  tax area this could make the interest  earned from these bonds far more  valuable for example if you're taxed  forty percent a municipal bond that pays  back three percent would actually be  equivalent to a regular investment  paying back five percent anyway moving  on to corporate bonds this is where  you'll find the most amount of  competition because companies generally  offer the highest interest rates and  they issue a limited number of bonds but  if you stick with large corporations  like apple who regularly issue billions  of dollars worth of bonds it shouldn't  be too hard to get your hands on these  either  alright now that we've discussed the  basic premise of bonds let's take a look  at why they're worth owning by far the  biggest advantage of bonds and  especially u.s treasury bonds is their  safety in fact treasury bonds are so  safe that the interest earned from these  bonds are considered risk-free i mean  this shouldn't be that surprising given  that a country like the us has virtually  no risk of default because they can just  print money to pay back their bond  holders this means that you're basically  guaranteed to make money if you simply  hold to maturity and this is why  marketable securities are generally just  considered cash  now if you try to exit your investment  early you may be looking at a loss  depending on how the bond market is  doing at a given time but if you hold to  a maturity it's basically like earning  interest from a savings account which is  unfortunately non-existent nowadays  corporate bonds on the other hand are by  no means risk-free but one positive is  that the risk is always clearly defined  you can look at a company's annual  earnings their profit margins and their  balance sheet to get a pretty good idea  of how likely they are to pay back their  debt and if you don't want to do that  you can take a look at risk ratings from  reputable companies like snp or moody's  usually these ratings separate corporate  bonds into two sections which are  investment grade bonds and  non-investment grade bonds for our  purposes we're mainly going to focus on  investment grade bonds these ratings are  super stringent and aaa ratings are  basically never handed out in fact  there's only three corporations in the  entire world that have a aaa rating  which are apple microsoft and johnson  johnson also it should be noted that  apple didn't even join this list till  the end of last year something else to  note is that if these companies do  collapse for some reason you're one of  the first people who are paid out after  liquidation according to section 507 of  the bankruptcy code the first people who  are paid out are secured debt holders  who have collateral but after these guys  are paid out bond holders are next in  line meanwhile shareholders are last in  line and they're usually not paid very  much if anything so if you're looking  for safety stability and security bonds  are definitely the way to go  one of the biggest criticisms against  bonds is that their yields are not that  high nowadays but i don't exactly think  that this is a bad thing sure bond  yields will never be as high as stock  returns but that's because you're taking  on substantially more risk and  volatility by investing in stocks all  assets are directly correlated to risk  they carry so it's not fair to say that  bonds as a whole are not worth it simply  because they offer lower returns than  stocks not to mention it there's plenty  of bonds that offer great yields if you  do your research likely the most  underrated bond in the entire world is  i-series bonds i-series bonds are  inflation-protected bonds that are  issued by the u.s government and they're  specifically designed to ensure that you  make a return even after accounting for  inflation and the given that inflation  is kai high right now so are yields on  eye bonds right now i bonds are paying  out a whopping 9.62 percent and you have  up until october to buy these interest  rates  if you didn't notice that's literally  higher than the average return on the s  p 500 now of course if inflation goes  down so will the returns on i bonds but  you'll never be losing money to  inflation in other words this is a  perfect store of value that's backed by  the us government the only catch is that  you can only buy ten thousand dollars  worth of i bonds every calendar year but  for the average person if you're also  maxing out your 401k and roth ira every  year and building up a larger emergency  fund like any responsible person should  do it this limit is likely more than  enough also if you do want to buy more  bonds there's plenty of solid options if  you do some digging take this oracle  bond for instance this bond matures in  may of 2025 and at current prices you  can lock in a yield over four percent  and that's just for holding three years  if you're willing to lock in for a  longer period like 20 years you can buy  the 2039 oracle bond that currently  offers a 5.79 yield some other high  yielding investment grade issuers  including mcdonald's atnt boeing and  fedex so clearly if you look deep enough  you can still find bonds that yield over  five percent  one of the biggest shortfalls of most  arguments against bonds is that they  almost always compare bonds to stocks or  real estate but the flaw with this  argument is that bonds don't have to be  treated as replacement to stock or real  to investing rather they could be  treated as a replacement to cash  after all basically every major  corporation out there holds the vast  majority of their cash in marketable  securities also regardless of how low  bond yields go they will always be  higher than the yields offered in  savings accounts now i'm not suggesting  that people hoard cash just so they can  invest in bonds the number one thing  that the average person should be doing  with access savings is investing in the  s p 500 however this doesn't mean that  there's no room for bonds there's plenty  of use cases in which bonds make a lot  more sense than cash  one example is saving up for a house  right now the median price of a house in  the us is 428 thousand dollars let's say  this is your first house and you're  looking to buy right underneath the  median at four hundred thousand dollars  to come up with a 20 down payment you  would need 80 000  there's also closing costs moving costs  and new furniture expenses so let's say  you budget out 100 000 for this new  house and to achieve this you're looking  to save 20 000 per year for five years  if you were to simply leave this money  in a savings account you might be able  to earn half a percent interest per year  at this rate you would earn about 1200  in interest by the time you buy the  house now what if you invested in boeing  bonds instead  boeing obviously isn't going anywhere in  the next five years and you can earn  about a five percent deal with this  boeing may 297 bond this yield will  slowly fall with time as we get closer  to maturity so while you may be locking  in a five percent deal during your first  12 months of saving you'll probably only  be locking in at one percent yield  during your last one months of saving to  keep things simple let's just say that  you average a three percent yield  throughout the next five years  at this rate you would earn seven  thousand six hundred dollars by the time  you're ready to buy your house that  itself could be enough to pay for your  closing costs and this logic doesn't  just apply to saving for a house either  it makes sense whenever you need money  on a set date or whenever you have  access cash reserves maybe you have  fifty thousand dollars set aside for a  car but you're waiting for the next  month to release in six or twelve months  putting this money in reliable bonds  would be way better than just holding  cash  clearly while bonds may not be a great  substitute for stocks or real estate  they can be a phenomenal substitute for  cash  despite all these benefits many people  including even warren buffett claim that  bonds aren't that useful in the current  market environment but the thing to note  about virtually everyone who claims this  is that they're not comparable to the  average person warren buffett has  averaged 20.5 percent per year from his  stock investments since 1965  so he's obviously not going to be  thrilled about a bond that's paying 5  per year but for the average person who  isn't a stock market genius and may not  even have any investments at all bonds  could be a great alternative to just  having all of their money in a savings  account also bonds could be a great  introduction to investing in general the  everyday person is usually intimidated  by the stock market because earning  reports and balance sheets seem complex  and hard to understand but bonds are  extremely straightforward you loan money  to the government or the most reputable  companies in the world and they pay you  back with interest by a certain date as  long as you hold to maturity you don't  have to worry about volatility or  fluctuating interest rates or any of  that and starting to earn money through  bonds may be exactly what the average  person needs to make the plunge into  stocks and take investing as a whole  more seriously and that's why i think  all americans should be investing in  bonds but that's just what i think would  you guys invest in bonds comment that  down below  also drop a like if you think eye bonds  are underrated and of course consider  joining our discord community to suggest  future video ideas and consider the  subscribe and see more questions  logically answered but until then i'm  hari and i'll see you guys on the next  one

